{
  "@context": {
    "schema": "https://schema.org/"
  },
  "@type": "Note",
  "@id": "note:notion-165",
  "title": "Concepts (v1)",
  "author": "person:jane-brew:001",
  "status": "draft",
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "b-1",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 1,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Concepts (v1)"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-3",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-2",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Theme 1: Deconstructing \"Raceblindness\" in France:** This theme centers on **Trica Keaton's critical examination of France's ideology of \"raceblind republicanism\" and its impact on the lived experiences of Black individuals**. The art direction could visually represent the tension between the official narrative of colorblindness and the reality of everyday antiblackness. This could be achieved through:"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-4",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Contrasting visuals:** Juxtaposing imagery that evokes French universalist ideals (e.g., iconic landmarks, symbols of the republic) with visuals that highlight the Black French experience and the realities of racial discrimination (as suggested by the hashtag #YouKnowYoureblackInFranceWhen...)."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-5",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Layered design:** Incorporating subtle layers or overlays that symbolize the hidden or often unacknowledged aspects of race and racism in French society."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-6",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Use of shadow and light:** Metaphorically representing the visibility and invisibility of Black French people within a \"raceblind\" context."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-7",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **A color palette that hints at or subtly incorporates diverse skin tones**, while perhaps maintaining an overall sophisticated and intellectual feel suitable for a scholar."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-9",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-8",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Theme 2: The Interwoven Tapestry of Afro/Black French Identity and the Diaspora:** This theme highlights **Professor Keaton's focus on Black French & European Studies, the Afro/Black Parisian experience, and its connection to the broader African diaspora**. The art direction could evoke a sense of historical depth, cultural richness, and interconnectedness. Consider:"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-10",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Imagery of Paris alongside diasporic elements:** Combining photographs or illustrations of Parisian cityscapes with motifs, patterns, or textures that resonate with African and African diasporic cultures. The \"Afro/Black Paris: The African Diaspora in the City of Light\" study program title itself provides strong visual inspiration."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-11",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Historical photographs or artistic representations:** Featuring or alluding to the historical presence and contributions of Black people in France, potentially referencing figures like Josephine Baker or the concept of \"Le Paris Noir\"."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-12",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **A color palette that is vibrant and inclusive**, reflecting the diversity of the African diaspora while maintaining an academic tone."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-13",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Visual elements that suggest movement and connection**, symbolizing the flow and exchange within the diaspora."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-15",
      "type": "bulletList",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "b-14",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "**Theme 3: Scholarly Inquiry and Critical Discourse:** This theme emphasizes **Trica Keaton's academic rigor, her interdisciplinary approach, and her engagement with critical race theory**. The art direction should convey intellectual authority and invite critical engagement with her work. This could involve:"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-16",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **A clean and sophisticated design:** Utilizing clear typography, ample white space, and a structured layout to promote readability and a sense of scholarly professionalism."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-17",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Abstract or conceptual imagery:** Employing visual metaphors that represent complex ideas related to race, identity, and power structures, rather than overly literal depictions."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-18",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **A more restrained color palette:** Perhaps using a base of neutral tones with strategic accents to highlight key information or thematic elements."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "b-19",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "- **Visual cues related to research and analysis:** Incorporating subtle design elements that suggest investigation, such as lines, grids, or fragmented imagery that invites closer examination."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}